# Where this file can live
#
# Any of these, and they are combined rather than overriding each other:
#
#   $ARGUS_PRIVATE_PATTERNS                    an explicit path
#   ~/.config/argus/private-patterns           per user - survives cloning
#   .private-patterns                          here, gitignored
#
# The repository copy is the obvious place and the weakest one: it lives
# in a single directory, so a fresh clone or a second worktree has none
# and the check quietly falls back to credentials only. Keep the per-user
# copy as well if you work in more than one checkout.
#
# Strings that must never reach this public repository.
#
# Copy to .private-patterns, which is gitignored, and fill in your own.
# Keep the real file local - putting these values in a committed file
# would publish exactly what you are trying to withhold.
#
# One extended regular expression per line; blank lines and # comments are
# ignored. Matching is case-insensitive.
#
# Every line here is checked at commit time and again before a push.

# Your employer, in the spellings that actually appear
# example-company
# examplecompany

# Internal hosts and services
# .*\.internal\.example\.com
# example\.atlassian\.net

# Identifier formats specific to your systems.
#
# Match the SHAPE, not a list of known values. A list only catches the
# ids you already know about, so a new colleague's would slip past every
# one of them - and listing real values here would publish exactly what
# you are trying to withhold.
# [0-9]{6}:[0-9a-f-]{8,}     an Atlassian account id

# Colleagues' names. Full names only: a lone first name matches ordinary
# words, and a check that cries wolf is one people learn to bypass.
# Firstname Lastname

# Credentials do NOT belong here. Issued-token prefixes and private key
# blocks are built into scripts/private-check.sh, so they are checked
# whether or not this file exists - a clone that protects nothing would
# be the worst possible default for the one mistake that cannot be taken
# back.
